## Proof that $L^p(X,\mathcal{A},\mu)$ is complete (Riesz–Fischer theorem)

Let $(X,\mathcal{A},\mu)$ be a measure space and let $1 \le p < \infty$. We prove that $L^p(\mu)$ is complete, i.e. every Cauchy sequence converges in $L^p$-norm. (The case $p=\infty$ is treated separately at the end.)

---

### Setup and reduction to an absolutely convergent subsequence

Let $(f_n)_{n\ge 1}$ be a Cauchy sequence in $L^p(\mu)$. It suffices to exhibit a subsequence converging in $L^p$ to some $f\in L^p$, because then the whole Cauchy sequence converges to the same limit (a Cauchy sequence with a convergent subsequence converges to the limit of that subsequence).

Since $(f_n)$ is Cauchy, choose indices $n_1 < n_2 < \cdots$ such that for all $m,n\ge n_k$,
$$\|f_m - f_n\|_p \le 2^{-k}.$$
In particular, setting $g_k := f_{n_k}$, we have
$$\|g_{k+1}-g_k\|_p \le 2^{-k}, \qquad k\ge 1. \tag{1}$$

---

### Construction of the candidate limit

Define the pointwise series
$$g(x) := g_1(x) + \sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\bigl(g_{k+1}(x)-g_k(x)\bigr).$$
We first show this series converges absolutely a.e. and defines an $L^p$ function. Consider the function
$$G(x) := |g_1(x)| + \sum_{k=1}^{\infty}|g_{k+1}(x)-g_k(x)|.$$
By the monotone convergence theorem (applied to the partial sums, which are nonnegative and increasing),
$$\int G^p\,d\mu = \lim_{N\to\infty}\int\Bigl(|g_1|+\sum_{k=1}^{N}|g_{k+1}-g_k|\Bigr)^p d\mu.$$
By the Minkowski inequality applied to the finite sum,
$$\Bigl(\int\Bigl(|g_1|+\sum_{k=1}^{N}|g_{k+1}-g_k|\Bigr)^p d\mu\Bigr)^{1/p} \le \|g_1\|_p + \sum_{k=1}^{N}\|g_{k+1}-g_k\|_p \le \|g_1\|_p + \sum_{k=1}^{N}2^{-k} \le \|g_1\|_p + 1.$$
Hence
$$\int G^p\,d\mu \le (\|g_1\|_p+1)^p < \infty.$$
Therefore $G\in L^p(\mu)$, and in particular $G(x)<\infty$ for $\mu$-a.e. $x$. At every such $x$, the series defining $g(x)$ converges absolutely, so $g(x)$ is well-defined. Set $g(x)=0$ on the null set where the series fails to converge. Then $|g(x)|\le G(x)$ a.e., so $g\in L^p(\mu)$.

---

### $L^p$-convergence of the subsequence

Let $S_N(x) := g_1(x) + \sum_{k=1}^{N-1}(g_{k+1}(x)-g_k(x)) = g_N(x)$. We have $|g(x)-S_N(x)|\le \sum_{k=N}^{\infty}|g_{k+1}(x)-g_k(x)|\le G(x)$, and $S_N\to g$ a.e. By the dominated convergence theorem (with dominating function $G^p\in L^1$),
$$\|g - g_N\|_p^p = \int|g-g_N|^p\,d\mu \longrightarrow 0.$$
Thus $g_N\to g$ in $L^p$.

---

### The whole Cauchy sequence converges to $g$

Given $\varepsilon>0$, pick $N$ with $\|f_m-f_n\|_p<\varepsilon$ for all $m,n\ge N$. For $n\ge N$ and any $k$ with $n_k\ge N$,
$$\|f_n - g\|_p \le \|f_n - g_k\|_p + \|g_k - g\|_p < \varepsilon + \|g_k-g\|_p.$$
Letting $k\to\infty$ (so $\|g_k-g\|_p\to 0$) gives $\|f_n-g\|_p\le\varepsilon$ for all $n\ge N$. Hence $f_n\to g$ in $L^p$.

---

### Case $p=\infty$

Let $(f_n)$ be Cauchy in $L^\infty$. For each $k$ pick $n_k$ with $\|f_m-f_n\|_\infty\le 2^{-k}$ for $m,n\ge n_k$. For each $k$ there is a null set $E_k$ with $|f_{n_{k+1}}(x)-f_{n_k}(x)|\le 2^{-k}$ off $E_k$. On $X\setminus\bigcup_k E_k$ the sequence $(f_{n_k})$ converges uniformly, hence pointwise, to a bounded measurable function $g$; extend by $0$ on the null union. Then $\|g - f_{n_k}\|_\infty\le\sum_{j\ge k}2^{-j}\to 0$, and the Cauchy argument above gives $f_n\to g$ in $L^\infty$.

---

### Conclusion

Every Cauchy sequence in $L^p(\mu)$ (for $1\le p\le\infty$) converges to a limit in $L^p(\mu)$. Therefore $L^p(\mu)$ is complete. $\blacksquare$

